clearcote-958: bind the CSS generics to the persona's clones on macOS (the CoreText equivalent
of fonts.conf.template's four <alias> lines).

assets/fonts/fonts.conf.template already does this for the Linux release:

  <alias><family>sans-serif</family><prefer><family>Arimo</family></prefer></alias>
  <alias><family>serif</family>     <prefer><family>Tinos</family></prefer></alias>
  <alias><family>monospace</family> <prefer><family>Inconsolata</family></prefer></alias>
  <alias><family>system-ui</family> <prefer><family>Selawik</family></prefer></alias>

macOS has no fontconfig, so those four lines have no effect there. The generics kept resolving
to the host's own defaults -- Helvetica, Times, Menlo, .AppleSystemUIFont -- which the persona
filter then hides, collapsing all of them onto the standard font.

MEASURED on the native macOS arm64 build of 150.0.7871.114, platform="windows", 32px "WgQmil":

  after `serif`      111.98
  after `monospace`  111.98     <- equal to serif
  after `cursive`    111.98     <- equal to serif
  `16px system-ui`    56.84     <- byte-identical to the macos persona

width(monospace) == width(serif) is impossible on real Windows, where the generics bind to
Times New Roman, Consolas and Arial respectively. It needs no reference data to detect, which
is the same class of tell the template's own comment was written to close:

    "system-ui had no rule at all, so it fell through to the default and measured identical to
     a family that does not exist, which is the cheapest possible tell"

TWO MAPPINGS ARE ENOUGH. The other two generics already arrive here as Windows families and
take the ordinary substitution path: macOS's sans-serif default is Helvetica, which 957
resolves as Arial -> Arimo, and its serif and standard default is Times, which is in the
Windows list -> Tinos. Both agree with the template.

  * system-ui reaches FontCache::GetFontPlatformData literally on macOS -- the system-ui
    shortcut at the top of that function is compiled out under IS_MAC and
    CreateFontPlatformData goes straight to MatchSystemUIFont(). Resolved as "Segoe UI", whose
    clone is Selawik: the template's target.
  * macOS's monospace default is Menlo. Resolved as "Consolas", whose clone is Inconsolata:
    again the template's target, including its documented residual width error.

Gated on FontDescription::GenericFamily() so that `font-family: Menlo` on its own stays ABSENT
under a Windows persona; only a request carrying the monospace generic is remapped.
`font-family: Menlo, monospace` matches both and is remapped, which is the right answer anyway
-- on real Windows Menlo is absent and the generic supplies Consolas.

MEASURED AFTER, platform="windows", 32px "WgQmil" through each generic:

  serif       111.98   Tinos         (Times New Roman)
  sans-serif  113.77   Arimo         (Arial, via 957's Helvetica -> Arial)
  monospace    96.00   Inconsolata   (Consolas)
  cursive     106.82   Comic Neue    (Comic Sans MS)
  fantasy     106.82   Comic Neue    (Papyrus -- see below)
  16px system-ui 57.96 Selawik       (Segoe UI); 56.84 under the macos persona

ONE RESIDUAL, MEASURED NOT GLOSSED. cursive and fantasy both land on Comic Neue, so
width(cursive) == width(fantasy) where genuine Windows has Comic Sans MS and Impact and they
differ. `fantasy` is NOT remapped on purpose: macOS's default for it is Papyrus, and Papyrus is
one of the families a real Windows machine HAS -- it is in the Windows list above -- so
remapping would make `font-family: Papyrus, fantasy` resolve to Impact where genuine Windows
resolves it to Papyrus. Trading a present font for an absent one is the worse of the two
errors. Closing this properly needs the generic to be distinguishable from an explicit first
family, which GenericFamily() alone does not give; left as a documented gap rather than traded
for a new one. The other four generics are now four distinct widths, where before this patch
serif, monospace and cursive were all 111.98. Emoji is also not bound: the open line has no emoji clone, since
MetricCompatibleSubstitute maps Segoe UI Emoji to Noto Color Emoji, which assets/fonts/ does
not ship and which CoreText cannot render in any case (it is a CBDT/CBLC bitmap font).

diff -u a/third_party/blink/renderer/platform/fonts/font_cache.cc b/third_party/blink/renderer/platform/fonts/font_cache.cc
--- a/third_party/blink/renderer/platform/fonts/font_cache.cc
+++ b/third_party/blink/renderer/platform/fonts/font_cache.cc
@@ -664,6 +664,87 @@
       // persona, and a same-OS persona, register nothing at all.
       EnsurePersonaSubstituteFontsRegistered();
 #endif
+#if BUILDFLAG(IS_MAC)
+      // clearcote-958: bind the CSS generics to the persona's clones, which is what
+      // assets/fonts/fonts.conf.template already does for the Linux release:
+      //
+      //   <alias><family>sans-serif</family><prefer><family>Arimo</family></prefer></alias>
+      //   <alias><family>serif</family>     <prefer><family>Tinos</family></prefer></alias>
+      //   <alias><family>monospace</family> <prefer><family>Inconsolata</family></prefer></alias>
+      //   <alias><family>system-ui</family> <prefer><family>Selawik</family></prefer></alias>
+      //
+      // macOS has no fontconfig, so those four lines have no effect here and the generics
+      // kept resolving to the host's defaults -- which the persona filter then hides,
+      // collapsing every one of them onto the standard font. MEASURED on the native
+      // 150.0.7871.114 arm64 build before this patch, platform="windows": an unresolvable
+      // family rendered at 111.98 after `serif`, after `monospace` AND after `cursive`,
+      // i.e. width(monospace) == width(serif), which is impossible on real Windows and is a
+      // reference-free tell of exactly the kind the template's comment was written to close.
+      // `16px system-ui` was also byte-identical between the macos and windows personas.
+      //
+      // Two mappings are enough, because the other two already arrive here as Windows
+      // families and take the ordinary substitution path: macOS's sans-serif default is
+      // Helvetica, which 957 resolves as Arial -> Arimo, and its serif and standard default
+      // is Times, which is in the Windows list -> Tinos. Both agree with the template.
+      //
+      //  * system-ui reaches this function literally on macOS (the system-ui shortcut at the
+      //    top of GetFontPlatformData is compiled out under IS_MAC and
+      //    CreateFontPlatformData goes straight to MatchSystemUIFont()). Resolved as
+      //    "Segoe UI", whose clone is Selawik -- the template's target.
+      //  * The remaining generics arrive as the macOS default family for that generic, from
+      //    chrome/app/resources/locale_settings_mac.grd, and are remapped to what
+      //    locale_settings_win.grd gives the same generic -- Chromium's own per-platform
+      //    answer, read out of the tree rather than assumed:
+      //
+      //      generic     mac .grd         win .grd                    clone
+      //      monospace   Menlo            Courier New / Consolas      Inconsolata
+      //                                   (IDS_FIXED_FONT_FAMILY_ALT_WIN)
+      //      cursive     Apple Chancery   Comic Sans MS               Comic Neue
+      //
+      //    Consolas rather than Courier New: Chrome takes the _ALT_WIN string on modern
+      //    Windows, and fonts.conf.template's comment says the same from the other side
+      //    ("monospace binds to Consolas on Windows, not Courier New", measured against
+      //    genuine Windows at 549.81).
+      //
+      //    Gated on GenericFamily() so an explicit `font-family: Menlo` stays ABSENT under a
+      //    Windows persona; only a request carrying that generic is remapped. `font-family:
+      //    Menlo, monospace` matches both and IS remapped, which is the right answer anyway:
+      //    on real Windows Menlo is absent and the generic supplies Consolas. The same holds
+      //    for Apple Chancery, which no Windows edition ships either.
+      //
+      // TWO THINGS DELIBERATELY NOT BOUND HERE.
+      //
+      //  * `fantasy`. macOS's default is Papyrus and Windows' is Impact, so a Windows
+      //    persona currently measures Papyrus's clone (Comic Neue) where genuine Windows
+      //    measures Impact's (Anton). It is NOT remapped, because Papyrus is one of the
+      //    families a real Windows machine HAS -- it is in the Windows list above -- so
+      //    remapping would make `font-family: Papyrus, fantasy` resolve to Impact where
+      //    genuine Windows resolves it to Papyrus. Trading a present font for an absent one
+      //    is the worse error of the two. Left as a measured, documented residual.
+      //  * Emoji. The open line has no emoji clone: MetricCompatibleSubstitute maps Segoe UI
+      //    Emoji to Noto Color Emoji, which assets/fonts/ does not ship and which CoreText
+      //    cannot render in any case (it is a CBDT/CBLC bitmap font).
+      if (spoofed_platform == "windows") {
+        const char* cc_generic = nullptr;
+        if (base::EqualsCaseInsensitiveASCII(requested_family, "system-ui")) {
+          cc_generic = "Segoe UI";
+        } else if (font_description.GenericFamily() ==
+                       FontDescription::kMonospaceFamily &&
+                   base::EqualsCaseInsensitiveASCII(requested_family, "Menlo")) {
+          cc_generic = "Consolas";
+        } else if (font_description.GenericFamily() ==
+                       FontDescription::kCursiveFamily &&
+                   base::EqualsCaseInsensitiveASCII(requested_family,
+                                                    "Apple Chancery")) {
+          cc_generic = "Comic Sans MS";
+        }
+        if (cc_generic) {
+          return GetFontPlatformData(font_description,
+                                     FontFaceCreationParams(AtomicString(cc_generic)),
+                                     alternate_font_name);
+        }
+      }
+#endif
       // Cross-OS persona: this host physically lacks the persona OS's fonts, so a requested
       // persona font would otherwise render absent/mismatched. Render each persona font
       // through a metric-compatible clone that IS installed here, so the family is present
